From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scott Jordan <scott@spaceshipcreative.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 05:22:32 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 12/25] Add opt-in Triton sparse MLA path for SM90 backend on
 GB10

Source: kindlingai/glm-5.3-flash-gx10 (Kindling) at c748079,
experimental/fixes/gb10_sparse_mla.py and the VLLM_TRITON_SPARSE_MLA
branch of experimental/fixes/flashinfer_mla_sparse_sm90.py, by Matt
Mastracci. The repository carries no LICENSE file; contributed here with
the owner's permission. The decode split follows Flash-Decoding (Dao et
al., 2023).
Upstream status: absent (no vLLM PR).

FlashInfer's FA2 MLA kernel is the only FlashInfer path the SM90 sparse
backend has on SM12x, and on GB10 it fits only a small KV tile. Add the
Kindling Triton kernel as an alternative for NoPE MLA: one program per
query token keeps every head's Q row in registers and streams the
token's top-k latent rows (FP8 E4M3 or BF16, one tensor-wide scale)
through tl.dot; small batches split each token's rows across programs
and combine the partial softmax state.

VLLM_TRITON_SPARSE_MLA=1 sends every forward_mqa row (decode, top-k
prefill and CUDA-graph replays) through the kernel and skips the
FlashInfer plan() in the builder, since the kernel reads per-row valid
counts on device. It is ignored with a warning on rope MLA
(qk_rope_head_dim != 0). Default off, so FlashInfer stays the default
for the backend.

The kernel file is Kindling's, reformatted to the vLLM line length and
importing triton through vllm.triton_utils; its AST is unchanged. The
other deltas in Kindling's backend copy duplicate the SM12x enablement
and the fp8 plan dtype already carried in earlier commits.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
